    "result_text": "The full suite is green (2/2 tests pass, BUILD SUCCESS).\n\n## Summary\n\nThe spec required exposing a computed `membershipTier` on the owner response: `SILVER` when `namesakeCount` is 0 and an email is present, else `BRONZE`.\n\nThe `GET /owners/{ownerId}` pipeline responds with an `OwnerDto` (generated from `openapi.yml`, populated by `OwnerMapper`), and the entity already carries `namesakeCount` and `email`. So I followed the exact pattern already used for the other computed fields (`displayName`, `membershipNumber`) rather than adding new code paths:\n\n1. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added a read-only `membershipTier` string property to the `Owner` schema, so the generated `OwnerDto` gains the field.\n2. **`OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 added one `@Mapping(target = \"membershipTier\", expression = ...)` line on the existing `toOwnerDto` method:\n   ```java\n   Integer.valueOf(0).equals(owner.getNamesakeCount()) && owner.getEmail() != null ? \"SILVER\" : \"BRONZE\"\n   ```\n   Null-safe: a null `namesakeCount` (never equal to 0) or null email yields `BRONZE`.\n\n**Structural impact kept low:** no method bodies were grown and no large class (like `Owner`) was touched. The only changed function is the annotation-only interface method `toOwnerDto` (CC 1), and the logic lives in a declarative mapping expression consistent with the surrounding two computed fields \u2014 a single cohesive addition rather than new branching in existing code.",
